{"prompt": "My flight got cancelled and I have a meeting in Berlin tomorrow morning. What do I do?", "response": "Let's fix this step by step. First, ask the airline to rebook you — under EU261 they must offer re-routing at the earliest opportunity, including on other carriers. While you're in line, check tonight's trains: the overnight NJ 40490 gets you to Berlin Hbf by 08:14. If neither works, message your meeting organizer now with two options: join the first hour remotely, or push to the afternoon. Want me to draft that message?"}
{"prompt": "How do I stop my sourdough from going flat when I bake it?", "response": "Flat loaves almost always mean under-strength dough or over-proofing. Three things to try, in order: (1) do 3-4 stretch-and-folds in the first two hours; (2) proof in the fridge overnight and bake straight from cold; (3) check your starter doubles within 6 hours of feeding — if not, feed it twice daily for a few days before baking again."}
{"prompt": "I just started learning Python and I'm stuck. My loop is printing None after every result. What's happening?", "response": "You're probably using print() inside a function that also returns something — and you're printing the function call itself. For example, `print(my_func())` will show the function's output plus None if the function doesn't explicitly return a value. Quick fix: either call `my_func()` alone (without print), or add `return result` at the end of your function. Want me to look at the specific code?"}
{"prompt": "I'm trying to cook risotto for the first time. The recipe says to stir constantly — what happens if I can't watch it the whole time?", "response": "Constant stirring releases starch for that creamy texture, but here's a practical compromise: stir every 2-3 minutes and you'll get 90% of the creaminess. If you truly can't tend it, the oven method works well — bring rice and broth to a simmer in a Dutch oven, cover, and bake at 350°F (175°C) for 30 minutes, stirring once halfway through. Finish with butter and parmesan off heat. The key is using enough broth (about 3:1 ratio to rice) and not skimping on the finish."}
{"prompt": "My toddler refuses to wear shoes and throws them off immediately. What can I do?", "response": "This is incredibly common and usually about sensory sensitivity or autonomy, not defiance. Try three approaches: (1) let them choose between two shoe options — the choice matters more than the shoes; (2) for sensory issues, try seamless socks first, then very soft slip-ons with no tags or elastic; (3) use a 'shoes on to go, off when we're back' rule with a visual timer. One trick that works for many parents: put a sticker on the tongue of the shoe so they can 'help' you by making sure it's 'facing up.' Does your child seem bothered by the texture, or is it more about not wanting them on?"}
{"prompt": "I need to write a resignation letter but I want to leave on good terms. Any tips?", "response": "Keep it brief, professional, and positive. Here's a template you can adapt:\n\n'Dear [Manager],\n\nI'm writing to formally resign from my position as [role], effective [date, typically 2 weeks out].\n\nI've genuinely enjoyed my time at [company] and am grateful for the opportunities I've had to [mention one specific thing]. I want to ensure a smooth transition and am happy to help train my replacement or document my workflows before I leave.\n\nThank you again for everything. I hope we can stay in touch.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your name]'\n\nSend it to your manager first in person or on a call, then follow up with the email. Want me to help tailor it to your specific situation?"}
{"prompt": "My apartment smells like cigarettes even though no one smokes inside. Neighbor's smoke is coming through the vents. What can I do?", "response": "This is a known issue — building ventilation can carry smoke from floor to floor. Here's what actually works: (1) Get a HEPA air purifier with an activated carbon filter for your space; (2) seal vent grilles with foam insulation tape and a rigid vent cover — this reduces airflow without creating pressure issues; (3) place a towel at the bottom of your door when you know neighbors are smoking. For the longer term, document everything (dates, times, photos of any smoke damage) and request a ventilation inspection from management — many buildings have to meet air quality standards. Have you tried talking to the neighbors yet?"}
{"prompt": "I'm a teacher and one student keeps interrupting the whole class. I've tried sending them to the back but they just disrupt from there. Help?", "response": "Sending a disruptive student to the back often backfires — they have more freedom to act out with an audience. Instead, try this sequence: First, proximity — stand near them while teaching without directly addressing them; it often self-corrects the behavior. Second, use 'redirect and re-engage' — acknowledge the comment briefly ('I hear you have something to add'), then set a clear boundary ('Please raise your hand so I can hear it after everyone has'). Third, after class, have a private, non-confrontational check-in: 'I've noticed you have lots of great ideas. I want to make sure everyone hears them. What would help you share at the right time?' If this continues, loop in the counselor early — they may have something going on. Want to brainstorm specific scripts for your classroom context?"}
{"prompt": "How do I start a small emergency fund when I'm barely covering expenses right now?", "response": "You don't need hundreds to start — the goal is building the habit and creating a real buffer. Here's a practical path: Step 1: Aim for $400 first (that's what the CBO found helps low-income households avoid the worst debt). Step 2: Find one hidden dollar per day — round up your grocery spend, sell one unused item, or skip one small subscription. Step 3: Put it in a separate account that doesn't have a debit card attached. Step 4: When you get the $400, keep going toward $1,000, then one month of essentials. The trick: track every penny in a shared doc or app and celebrate each $50 milestone. Can we work out what's realistically in your range to start?"}
